# examples/annotated_binding.ab
#
# A worked example of every line kind in the 0xab binding spec:
#   v1: import, alias, substitution
#   v2: citation chain (with @all / @once-per-doc flags)
#   v3: annotation (with #N positional + @margin/@endnote/@inline modes)
#
# This file is illustration only — not inscribed. The txids cited
# below are real corpus inscriptions; the anchors are example phrases
# that may or may not exist in any particular essay's body.
#
# To inscribe a binding like this:
#   from canonical.bindings import build_binding_quipu, TONE_AI
#   header, body = build_binding_quipu(open("annotated_binding.ab").read(),
#                                       tone=TONE_AI)
#   # then broadcast via CadenaAtom + diamond pattern as usual.


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# v1 — IMPORT another binding's rules (e.g. inherit a glossary).
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
<<8306eda8433bb95d24977d62042b65aae664947c670046887d6662bd723eba3c>>


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# v1 — ALIAS CHAIN: give an inscription a project-local name.
# Multiple aliases on the left, single txid target on the right.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
<<Goethe>>=<<84fbbb17718523edf373630e68239fa9abda85297ba2aca8a69ace04f0ad5fb5>>
<<Cementerio>>=<<CementerioDeLosAnimales>>=<<449e67f4a2e948f044e1a32662b58ccedad3cf8e85a742cc45b6b6541e47b5d7>>


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# v1 — STRING SUBSTITUTION: pure literal rewrite, every occurrence.
# Use for typo fixes, terminology updates, URL repairs.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
"hebreo"="yiddish"
"https://dogechain.info/tx/"="quipu:"


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# v2 — CITATION CHAIN: prose terms become markdown citation links.
# Default: first occurrence per block, word-bounded, case-sensitive.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
"Goethe"=<<84fbbb17718523edf373630e68239fa9abda85297ba2aca8a69ace04f0ad5fb5>>

# Equivalence class — three surface forms all link to the same target,
# matched form is preserved as anchor text.
"Hayagriva"="hayagriva"="HAYAGRIVA"=<<aaaa1111aaaa1111aaaa1111aaaa1111aaaa1111aaaa1111aaaa1111aaaa1111>>

# Flags adjust default behavior.
"Bordado"=<<aaaa2222aaaa2222aaaa2222aaaa2222aaaa2222aaaa2222aaaa2222aaaa2222>> @all
"Christamicus"=<<aaaa3333aaaa3333aaaa3333aaaa3333aaaa3333aaaa3333aaaa3333aaaa3333>> @once-per-doc


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# v3 — ANNOTATIONS: multi-paragraph notes anchored to phrases in the
# essay body. The opening fence is `@@anchor [#N] [@flag ...]` on its
# own line; the closing fence is `@@` on its own line. Everything
# between is the note body as markdown.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# DEFAULT mode (margin): first occurrence per block, Tufte-style
# sidenote rendered in the margin alongside the anchor's line.
@@Yorkshire terrier named Beatrice
The dog's name carries the Dante echo throughout this essay's
cosmology. Beatrice as the guide through the spheres of paradise,
here as the guide through the cemetery. The author's wife (also a
Beatrice) and the dog share the name without ceremony.
@@


# ENDNOTE mode: superscript at anchor, collected into a "Notes"
# section at the end of the essay with a backlink to the anchor.
@@Operation Condor @endnote
The codename for the multinational intelligence-coordination
program among the security services of Argentina, Bolivia, Brazil,
Chile, Paraguay, and Uruguay, founded at a meeting in Santiago on
25 November 1975. The conference convened on Pinochet's sixtieth
birthday — the fusing of a head-of-state's private anniversary
with the public birth of a transnational killing program is not
incidental, and the essay's juxtaposition of biographical and
state-historical dates throughout deserves attention.

The CONDORTEL teletype-based encrypted communications channel
that the program ran on was not cryptographically novel; what was
hardened was the channel's *social topology* — who could speak to
whom, on what schedule, with what authorization. The Quipu
Protocol's multisignature address structure is, in a different
register, also a hardening of social topology against forgery.
@@


# INLINE mode: bracketed `[note]` inserted into the prose flow at
# the anchor point. Use sparingly — heavy use clutters reading.
@@Sparkle, Paco, and Beatrice were buried in the chain @inline
These three image inscriptions from May 2022 predate the v1
protocol by ~4 years; the project memory flags them as
"pre-canonical." The chain holds them regardless.
@@


# POSITIONAL #N: when the anchor phrase appears multiple times,
# `#N` picks the Nth occurrence (1-indexed). Useful for annotating
# the third Beatrice mention rather than the first.
@@Beatrice #3 @margin
By this third mention, the essay has shifted from referring to the
dog (and the cosmological Beatrice she shares a name with) to
the author's wife. The slippage is part of the essay's mode.
@@


# @all + presentation flag: every occurrence gets an annotation.
# Rare; use only when the phrase truly merits per-mention commentary.
@@chain @all @inline
Refers throughout to the Dogecoin blockchain on which all of this
is inscribed.
@@


# @once-per-doc + endnote: only the first occurrence in the entire
# document gets the note, no matter how many times the phrase appears.
@@Pet Sematary @once-per-doc @endnote
Stephen King's novel was written in 1979 and published in 1983,
the years bracketing the most active period of Condor. King has
said he found the manuscript so dark he initially refused to
publish it. There is no direct connection between the two
histories; they are juxtaposed here because juxtaposition is the
essay's mode. The chain places them next to each other and lets
the reader infer.
@@


# Multi-paragraph notes can include inline `code`, *italics*,
# **bold**, [links](https://example.com), citation references like
# <<txid>>, and blank lines between paragraphs. The @@…@@ fence is
# the boundary, not the blank-line convention.
@@gematría
The Hebrew system in which every letter carries a numerical value,
every word a sum, and every sum a possible reference. Structurally
it is a *pre-cryptographic addressing scheme* — letters and
numbers as a single substrate.

The Quipu Protocol's `<<txid>>` citation syntax inherits the same
conceptual move: every glyph in a 64-hex transaction id is at once
a letter, a number, and a reference. Reading the chain is reading
gematría with a wider alphabet.
@@
